3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-search size-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m21 21-4.34-4.34"></path><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle></svg><span class="hidden text-xs xl:inline">Search</span><kbd class="hidden rounded-sm bg-bg-subtle px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-2xs text-fg-subtle xl:inline">⌘K</kbd></button><span class="hidden h-11 items-center px-3 text-xs whitespace-nowrap text-fg-subtle sm:inline-flex" title="Static preview: sign-in runs on the server deployment">Static preview</span><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-state="closed" class="size-11 items-center justify-center rounded-md text-fg-muted hover:bg-bg-subtle hover:text-fg data-[state=on]:text-fg hidden sm:inline-flex" aria-label="Dark theme" data-overlay-trigger="_R_ul6_"><span class="relative size-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-sun absolute inset-0 size-4 transition-[opacity,transform,filter] duration-250 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)] scale-[0.25] opacity-0 blur-[4px]" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"></circle><path d="M12 2v2"></path><path d="M12 20v2"></path><path d="m4.93 4.93 1.41 1.41"></path><path d="m17.66 17.66 1.41 1.41"></path><path d="M2 12h2"></path><path d="M20 12h2"></path><path d="m6.34 17.66-1.41 1.41"></path><path d="m19.07 4.93-1.41 1.41"></path></svg><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-moon absolute inset-0 size-4 transition-[opacity,transform,filter] duration-250 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)] scale-100 opacity-100 blur-0" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20.985 12.486a9 9 0 1 1-9.473-9.472c.405-.022.617.46.402.803a6 6 0 0 0 8.268 8.268c.344-.215.825-.004.803.401"></path></svg></span></button><button type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" data-state="closed" class="inline-flex size-11 items-center justify-center rounded-md text-fg lg:hidden" data-overlay-trigger="menu"><span class="sr-only">Open menu</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-menu size-5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5h16"></path><path d="M4 12h16"></path><path d="M4 19h16"></path></svg></button></div></div></header><main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="flex-1"><!--$--><div class="border-b border-border"><div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pt-16 pb-14 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 sm:pb-20"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING · June 6, 2026 · 8 min read</p><h1 class="mt-5 max-w-4xl font-display text-4xl leading-[1.02] tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">Inside the WDBX Hybrid Ranker: Four Factors, One Score</h1><p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-lg leading-8 text-fg-muted">Semantic similarity is only the first of four factors. Here is the exact score WDBX uses to rank retrieval candidates — and why every factor is bounded to [0,1].</p><p class="mt-5 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-fg-muted">By <!-- -->MLAI Research · WDBX Core</p></div></div><section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 sm:py-28"><div class="max-w-3xl space-y-10"><article><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Plain top-k vector search answers one question: what is closest to the query in embedding space? That is a fine question, and a poor stopping point. A record can be a near-perfect semantic match and still be the wrong thing to retrieve — because it is two years stale, because it sits far from the query in the causal graph, or because the active persona should not be weighting it heavily.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The WDBX hybrid ranker, which companions our paper on the weighted-backtrace store, answers the harder question by combining four independent factors into a single bounded score. This note walks through that score the way you would read it during an incident review.</p></article><article><h2 id="the-four-factors" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">The four factors</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Every candidate j is ranked by the product of four terms, each living in the unit interval. Because they multiply, any one factor collapsing toward zero pulls the whole score down — there is no averaging away a fatal weakness:</p><ul class="mt-5 max-w-[66ch] space-y-3"><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>σ — semantic similarity (cosine distance between query and candidate, via a SIMD path with a deterministic CPU fallback).</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>τ — temporal weight (exponential recency decay).</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>γ — causal weight (proximity along the backtrace graph).</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>π — persona weight, supplied by the router for the active profile.</li></ul><div class="mt-4 space-y-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 px-4 py-3 text-fg"><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">s_{ij} \;=\; \sigma_j \,\cdot\, \tau_j \,\cdot\, \gamma_j \,\cdot\, \pi_j</div></div></article><article><h2 id="recency-and-relatedness-written-down" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Recency and relatedness, written down</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Recency is an exponential half-life decay: a record&#x27;s temporal weight halves every t½, clamped so future-dated records cannot exceed one. Causal weight decays with the number of graph hops h between the query focus and the candidate, but bottoms out at a floor so unrelated records are down-weighted, not erased:</p><div class="mt-4 space-y-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 px-4 py-3 text-fg"><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">\tau_j \;=\; \max\!\Big(0,\ \min\!\big(1,\ 2^{-(t_0 - t_j)/t_{1/2}}\big)\Big)</div><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">\gamma_j \;=\; \max\!\big(c_{\mathrm{floor}},\ c_{\mathrm{decay}}^{\,h_j}\big), \qquad c_{\mathrm{decay}} = 0.6,\ c_{\mathrm{floor}} = 0.25</div></div></article><article><h2 id="why-a-product-beats-a-weighted-sum" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Why a product beats a weighted sum</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">A weighted sum lets a high score on one axis paper over a near-zero on another — exactly the failure that produces confident, stale, off-topic retrievals. The product makes each factor a veto: low semantic support, ancient timestamps, or distant causal paths each independently sink the rank. That is the behaviour you want from a store whose whole point is to be defensible after the fact.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">And because the factors are stored alongside the rank, the ranker is its own explanation. You never have to reconstruct why a record surfaced — the four numbers that decided it are right there in the trace.</p></article></div><div class="mt-14 flex max-w-3xl flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-6 border-t border-border pt-8"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 border border-transparent font-medium whitespace-nowrap no-underline transition-[background-color,color,box-shadow,opacity,transform] duration-150 ease-out select-none focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ring disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50 active:not-disabled:translate-y-px bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/80 h-11 rounded-md px-4 text-sm" href="/contact">Talk to our engineers</a><a class="text-right no-underline" href="/blog/what-the-model-sees-sea"><span class="block text-xs text-fg-subtle">Next note</span><span class="mt-1 block font-display text-lg text-fg hover:underline">How Sparse Evidence Attention Decides What the Model Sees</span></a></div></section><section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6"><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 border border-transparent font-medium whitespace-nowrap no-underline transition-[background-color,color,box-shadow,opacity,transform] duration-150 ease-out select-none focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ring disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50 active:not-disabled:translate-y-px bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/80 h-11 rounded-md px-4 text-sm active" href="/blog" data-status="active" aria-current="page">All notes</a></div><div class="mt-10"><div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2"><a class="surface surface-hover p-5 no-underline" href="/research"><p class="text-xs text-accent">Next</p><h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-xl text-fg">Research</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">The papers these notes sit beside.</p></a></div></div></section><sc