22,1,0.36,1)] scale-100 opacity-100 blur-0" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20.985 12.486a9 9 0 1 1-9.473-9.472c.405-.022.617.46.402.803a6 6 0 0 0 8.268 8.268c.344-.215.825-.004.803.401"></path></svg></span></button><button type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" data-state="closed" class="inline-flex size-11 items-center justify-center rounded-md text-fg lg:hidden" data-overlay-trigger="menu"><span class="sr-only">Open menu</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-menu size-5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5h16"></path><path d="M4 12h16"></path><path d="M4 19h16"></path></svg></button></div></div></header><main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="flex-1"><!--$--><div class="border-b border-border"><div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pt-16 pb-14 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 sm:pb-20"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING · May 18, 2026 · 9 min read</p><h1 class="mt-5 max-w-4xl font-display text-4xl leading-[1.02] tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">What We Plan to Measure Before Calling an Agent Production-Ready</h1><p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-lg leading-8 text-fg-muted">Latency and accuracy are not enough. The gate we are building tracks tool-boundary violations, source coverage, rollback paths, abstention quality, and human-review burden.</p><p class="mt-5 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-fg-muted">By <!-- -->MLAI Safety Engineering</p></div></div><section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 sm:py-28"><div class="max-w-3xl space-y-10"><article><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Latency and accuracy are the metrics that demo well, which is exactly why they are insufficient as a release gate. An agent can be fast and frequently correct while still being unsafe to deploy, because the failure modes that matter in production are the ones that do not show up in a happy-path benchmark.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The Evaluation Mesh is our planned answer: the design turns AI quality into a release gate instead of an after-the-fact dashboard. It is not built yet. The bar we are building toward is that, before we call an agent production-ready, it has to clear regression scenarios across dimensions that a single accuracy score hides.</p></article><article><h2 id="the-dimensions-we-plan-to-gate-on" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">The dimensions we plan to gate on</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The design is that each of these runs as a repeatable scenario suite, not a one-time audit, and that a change to a prompt, a tool permission, or a retrieval index re-runs the full mesh.</p><ul class="mt-5 max-w-[66ch] space-y-3"><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>Tool-boundary violations — did the agent attempt an action outside its granted permissions, even if the attempt was blocked?</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>Source coverage — what fraction of factual claims trace back to a retrieved record versus model memory?</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>Rollback paths — for every write action, can we reconstruct and reverse it from trace artifacts alone?</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>Abstention quality — when the agent should have said &quot;I don&#x27;t know&quot; or escalated, did it? Confident wrong answers are scored worse than honest abstentions.</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>Prompt-injection resilience — does adversarial content in retrieved documents or user input change the agent&#x27;s permissions or goals?</li><li class="flex gap-3 text-base leading-8 text-fg"><span class="mt-3 size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>Human-review burden — how many runs require an operator, and is that number trending toward fatigue?</li></ul></article><article><h2 id="abstention-is-a-feature-not-a-failure" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Abstention is a feature, not a failure</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Most evaluation harnesses penalize a model for not answering. Our design inverts that for high-stakes workflows. An agent that abstains and escalates when retrieval confidence is low is behaving correctly; an agent that fabricates a plausible answer under the same conditions should fail the gate, regardless of how often it is right elsewhere.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">This is why the persona split matters operationally. Abbey&#x27;s analytical, safety-oriented review is meant to flag a low-confidence retrieval before Abi&#x27;s action-oriented execution profile ever touches production data.</p></article><article><h2 id="review-burden-as-a-leading-indicator" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Review burden as a leading indicator</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The metric teams most often forget is the one that predicts whether their controls will survive contact with reality: human-review burden. If every run needs a human, the system does not scale. If no run needs a human, the gates are probably theater. The plan is to track the ratio over time and treat a sharp move in either direction as a signal that the control plane needs retuning, not just the model.</p></article></div><div class="mt-14 flex max-w-3xl flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-6 border-t border-border pt-8"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 border border-transparent font-medium whitespace-nowrap no-underline transition-[background-color,color,box-shadow,opacity,transform] duration-150 ease-out select-none focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ring disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50 active:not-disabled:translate-y-px bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/80 h-11 rounded-md px-4 text-sm" href="/contact">Talk to our engineers</a><a class="text-right no-underline" href="/blog/vector-search-to-backtrace-graphs"><span class="block text-xs text-fg-subtle">Next note</span><span class="mt-1 block font-display text-lg text-fg hover:underline">From Vector Search to Weighted Backtrace Graphs</span></a></div></section><section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6"><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 border border-transparent font-medium whitespace-nowrap no-underline transition-[background-color,color,box-shadow,opacity,transform] duration-150 ease-out select-none focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ring disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50 active:not-disabled:translate-y-px bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/80 h-11 rounded-md px-4 text-sm active" href="/blog" data-status="active" aria-current="page">All notes</a></div><div class="mt-10"><div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2"><a class="surface surface-hover p-5 no-underline" href="/research"><p class="text-xs text-accent">Next</p><h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-xl text-fg">Research</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">The papers these notes sit beside.</p></a></div></div></section><script>(function(a,f){let l;try{l=JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem(a)||"{}")}