d hover:text-fg" href="/research/implementations/quasar-bounded-repository-generation">Quasar as a bounded repository-generation loop</a></li><li><a class="flex min-h-10 items-center rounded-md px-2 text-sm no-underline transition-colors text-fg-muted hover:bg-muted hover:text-fg" href="/research/implementations/abbey-executable-capability-ledger">Abbey capability claims as an executable ledger</a></li><li><a class="flex min-h-10 items-center rounded-md px-2 text-sm no-underline transition-colors text-fg-muted hover:bg-muted hover:text-fg" href="/research/implementations/wdbx-specimen-architecture-and-conformance">WDBX Specimen separates architecture, runtime profiles, and proof</a></li><li><a class="flex min-h-10 items-center rounded-md px-2 text-sm no-underline transition-colors text-fg-muted hover:bg-muted hover:text-fg" href="/research/implementations/deterministic-research-provenance-exports">Deterministic research exports with citation gates</a></li></ul></div></nav><div><div class="mb-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2"><article class="surface accent-edge p-6"><p class="text-xs text-accent">Practical summary</p><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">Prototype project recall or document assistance without placing every available record in a prompt.</p></article><article class="surface accent-edge p-6"><p class="text-xs text-accent">Status</p><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">The described source behavior is implemented; customer outcomes and deployment readiness are not established.</p><p class="mt-2 font-mono text-2xs text-fg-subtle">Reviewed <!-- -->2026-09-30</p></article></div><nav aria-label="Related products" class="mb-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm"><a class="text-accent" href="/products">All products</a></nav><div class="max-w-3xl space-y-10"><article><h2 id="practical-context" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Practical context</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">A source-backed account of eight-signal selection and bounded prompt assembly.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Prototype project recall or document assistance without placing every available record in a prompt.</p></article><article><h2 id="implementation-and-research-boundary" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Implementation and research boundary</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">SEA combines semantic, keyword, metadata, recency, authority, graph, contradiction and task-fit signals. Task-aware scoring adjusts selected task weights before bounded selection. The learning loop separately persists adaptive persona-router weights; these are not evidence that the eight retrieval weights were trained on a quality benchmark.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The recall path estimates token cost from bytes and limits the assembled preamble by bytes. Task-aware retrieval scoring and the persisted adaptive persona router are separate mechanisms. No measured answer-quality improvement is asserted.</p></article><article><h2 id="score-construction-and-its-interpretation" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Score construction and its interpretation</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The eight dimensions are semantic, keyword, metadata, recency, authority, graph, contradiction and task fit, in that order. Default weights sum to one. Code-repair, project-recall and benchmark-review adjustments are additive and are not renormalized by adjust_weights_for_task; the final score is clamped. A score of one is therefore a saturated heuristic, not calibrated confidence. In generic-store recall, self-asserted authority metadata is observed but not trusted: those candidates are scored with inferred authority.</p><div class="mt-4 space-y-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 px-4 py-3 text-fg"><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">s(c)=\mathrm{clamp}_{[0,1]}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{8}w_i x_i(c)\right)</div><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">w=(0.30,0.15,0.15,0.10,0.10,0.10,0.05,0.05)</div></div></article><article><h2 id="selection-under-several-budgets" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Selection under several budgets</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">The selector orders candidates by descending score and breaks ties by stable record identifier. Duplicate identifiers do not consume budget twice. A candidate is rejected when the record cap or estimated token budget would be exceeded. The per-cluster diversity cap has an explicit exception for scores at least 0.92; it is not an unconditional diversity guarantee. Returned selected and rejected identifier lists share an aggregate reason, not a detailed per-candidate explanation.</p><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Token estimates use snippet byte length divided by four, rounded upward with a minimum of one. Prompt construction applies its own byte limit afterward. For example, under a ten-token budget, candidates costing eight and five estimated tokens cannot both fit; greedy order may select the first and reject the second even if another combination would be preferable.</p><div class="mt-4 space-y-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 px-4 py-3 text-fg"><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">\widehat{T}(x)=\max(1,\lceil |x|_{\mathrm{bytes}}/4\rceil)</div><div tabindex="0" class="my-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-bg-subtle px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-fg-subtle">\sum_{c\in S}\widehat{T}(c)\leq B</div></div></article><article><h2 id="evaluate-retrieval-and-generation-separately" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Evaluate retrieval and generation separately</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Build fixtures for duplicate hits, tied scores, absent timestamps, self-asserted authority, conflicting records and an empty store. Keyword overlap counts significant query tokens matching case-insensitive substrings; it is not a unique-set intersection metric. Recency is relative to the latest candidate timestamp, not automatically wall-clock age. Inspect selected identifiers and the final prompt bytes before evaluating the resulting answer with a separate rubric. The EMA router update in the learning loop does not establish that evidence relevance improves over time.</p></article><article><h2 id="evaluation-before-adoption" class="scroll-mt-24 font-display text-[1.65rem] leading-tight tracking-tight text-fg">Evaluation before adoption</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-base leading-8 text-fg">Use a documented corpus with relevant, irrelevant, conflicting and missing evidence. Review selected context and bounded behavior alongside answer quality; a successful selection test alone is not a hallucination-reduction result.</p></article></div><h3 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Limitations</h3><div class="mt-3"><ul class="grid gap-3 text-sm text-fg-muted sm:grid-cols-2"><li class="surface px-4 py-3">Token costs are estimates; the implementation also enforces a prompt-byte boundary.</li><li class="surface px-4 py-3">Scoring heuristics do not guarantee relevance, truth or improved answer quality.</li><li class="surface px-4 py-3">The recall path estimates token cost from bytes and limits the assembled preamble by bytes. Task-aware retrieval scoring and the persisted adaptive persona router are separate mechanisms. No measured answer-quality improvement is asserted.</li></ul></div><div class="mt-10 grid gap-8"><div><h3 class="font-display text-xl">Supporting sources</h3><ul class="mt-3 space-y-3"><li class="surface p-4"><a class="text-sm font-medium text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/abi">Bounded evidence recall and prompt assembly</a><p class="mt-1 text-xs text-fg-muted">source<!-- --> · revision<!-- --> <code class="break-all font-mono">80dfe079ebe7413a6815c2d564be1f0aaa901267</code></p></li><li class="surface p-4"><a class="text-sm font-medium text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/abi">Eight-signal selection and task weights</a><p class="mt-1 text-xs text-fg-muted">source<!-- --> · revision<!-- --> <code class="break-all font-mono">80dfe079ebe7413a6815c2d564be1f0aaa901267</code></p></li><li class="surface p-4"><a class="text-sm font-medium text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/abi">Learning loop and persisted router weights</a><p class="mt-1 text-xs text-fg-muted">source<!-- --> · revision<!-- --> <code class="break-all font-mono">80dfe079ebe7413a6815c2d564be1f0aaa901267</code></p></li><li class="surface p-4"><a class="text-sm font-medium text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/abi">Keyword-based task classification</a><p class="mt-1 text-xs text-fg-muted">source<!-- --> · revision<!-- --> <code class="break-all font-mono">80dfe079ebe7413a6815c2d564be1f0aaa901267</code></p></li></ul></div></div><div class="mt-12 grid gap-8"><div><h3 class="font-display text-xl">More on this track</h3><ul class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><li><a class="text-sm text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/research/sea-overview">SEA evidence selection</a></li><li><a class="text-sm text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/research/backtrace-confidence-signals-hallucination">Backtrace Confidence Signals for Hallucination Reduction</a></li><li><a class="text-sm text-accent no-underline hover:underline" href="/research/chunk-provenance-long-context-retrieval">Chunk Provenance in Long-Context Retrie